# The smallest ruleset that proves 'nft --check' can run here.
#
# '--check' parses a file without committing it, but before it reads a byte it
# opens a netlink socket and populates its cache, and that needs CAP_NET_ADMIN.
# An *empty* file does not: nft has nothing to resolve, never initialises the
# cache, and exits 0 in a process that could not have checked anything. So an
# empty file is not a probe. This one declares a table, which is enough to make
# nft consult the cache and therefore enough to fail with "cache initialization
# failed: Operation not permitted" when the capability is missing.
#
# Two readers, deliberately the same bytes:
#
#   * tests/platform_marks.py runs it to decide whether the nft gate in
#     tests/test_firewall.py can run at all, and skips those tests if it cannot.
#   * .github/workflows/ci.yml runs it after granting the capability, so a grant
#     that did not take fails the job loudly instead of quietly turning the gate
#     off for the rest of the run.
#
# Nothing here is committed and no table of this name is created: --check stops
# before the transaction. Keep it to constructs every nft since 0.9 accepts.
table inet netviz_probe {
	chain c {
		type filter hook input priority 0;
	}
}
